# Line endings are LF everywhere — in the repository and in the working tree
# alike — and this is pinned rather than checked. `text=auto eol=lf` makes a CRLF
# checkout impossible whatever a contributor's `core.autocrlf` says, which is a
# local setting the repository cannot see and must not depend on. Pinning beats
# checking: there is nothing left for a hook to detect.
#
# This is only half the rule for Rust. rustfmt defaults to
# `newline_style = "Auto"`, under which a CRLF file can survive formatting
# untouched and `cargo fmt --check` reports it clean. rustfmt.toml pins
# `newline_style = "Unix"` so that `cargo fmt` normalises to LF and `--check`
# treats CRLF as the formatting error it is. Neither half replaces the other.
#
# To inspect: `git ls-files --eol` reports index and working tree separately.
# Grepping a file for carriage returns answers only the uninteresting half.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and corrupted by any end-of-line conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.jar binary
*.ico binary
